{#
  The trial queue table for one patient — the mirror of _patient_rows.html.twig.

  Expects: patient, trials (keyed by trial id), latest (evaluations keyed by
           trial id), counts, remaining, hasActive.

  ── The rule this table follows ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  Identical to the patient-first table, because it is the same rule read along
  the other axis. A row's action is DERIVED from the data, never remembered by
  the browser:

      no evaluation          → offer "Queue"
      pending / running      → show the status, offer nothing
      completed / failed     → show the status, offer "Re-run"

  So the button genuinely disappears after queueing and stays gone across a
  refresh, a back-navigation, or someone else queueing the same pairing in
  another tab.

  ── What the queue form posts ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Exactly what the trial-first table posts: trial_id, person_id and a CSRF token
  scoped to that pair, to the same /evaluations/queue endpoint. The only addition
  is the hidden `flow` field, which does nothing but tell the controller which
  screen to send you back to. Two ways in, one way to queue — which is what makes
  comparing the two flows a comparison of the interface rather than of the
  behaviour underneath it.
#}
{#
  data-active is the poller's stop signal, and it is deliberately INSIDE the
  frame: Turbo replaces a frame's children, never the frame element's own
  attributes, so a flag on the wrapper would freeze at its first-render value and
  the loop would never terminate.
#}
{#
  ── The match filter toggle ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Plain links, not a <select> with an onchange handler: three GETs to the same
  screen. That means it works with JavaScript off, each position is bookmarkable
  and back-buttonable, and there is no form to submit. A GET that changes only
  what you are looking at is exactly what a query string is for.

  Marked up as a nav with aria-current on the active position, so a screen reader
  announces which of the three is in effect rather than reading three
  indistinguishable links.

  NOTE (2026-08-26): EXACT's ?type= parameter is currently inert on this
  deployment — all three positions return identical trial sets and every result
  is labelled "eligible". The control is correct against the documented contract
  and will start discriminating once the marker tables are restored upstream.
  Three identical counts is the symptom; three different counts is the all-clear.
#}
{% if not matchingFailed %}
  <nav class="match-filter" aria-label="Match filter">
    <span class="muted" id="match-filter-label">Show:</span>
    <ul aria-labelledby="match-filter-label">
      {% for option in matchFilters %}
        <li>
          {% if option == matchFilter %}
            {# The current position is not a link — there is nowhere to go. #}
            <strong aria-current="true">{{ option.label }}</strong>
          {% else %}
            <a href="{{ path('lumina_ui_evaluation_new_by_patient', {patient: patient.personId, match: option.value}) }}"
               title="{{ option.description }}">{{ option.label }}</a>
          {% endif %}
        </li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
    {# The explanation of the ACTIVE position, always visible. `title` alone is
       unreachable by keyboard and unreliable on touch, so it is a supplement
       here and never the only copy of this text. #}
    <p class="muted">{{ matchFilter.description }}</p>
  </nav>
{% endif %}

<div class="status-summary"
     data-evaluation-poll-target="state"
     data-active="{{ hasActive ? 'true' : 'false' }}">
  {#
    ONE polite live region for the whole table, not one per cell — 180 trials
    means 180 individually-changing status cells, which is unusable noise in a
    screen reader. A single sentence saying what changed is genuinely useful.
  #}
  <p aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" class="muted">
    {% if counts.queued == 0 %}
      No evaluations run for this patient yet.
    {% else %}
      {{ counts.queued }} of {{ trials|length }} trial{{ trials|length == 1 ? '' : 's' }} ran ·
      {{ counts.active }} in progress ·
      {{ counts.completed }} completed{% if counts.failed > 0 %} · {{ counts.failed }} failed{% endif %}.
    {% endif %}
  </p>

  {#
    Bulk queue. Inside the frame, next to the counts it depends on, so the number
    on the button stays truthful as rows are queued elsewhere rather than going
    stale at whatever it was when the page first loaded.

    Only ever queues trials with NO evaluation yet — never re-runs a finished
    one — so the button is safe to press twice: the second press finds nothing
    remaining and says so.
  #}
  {% if remaining > 0 %}
    <form method="post" action="{{ path('lumina_ui_evaluation_queue_all_trials') }}"
          onsubmit="return confirm('Queue evaluations against {{ remaining }} trial{{ remaining == 1 ? '' : 's' }} for this patient?');">
      <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('queue-all-patient-' ~ patient.personId) }}">
      <input type="hidden" name="person_id" value="{{ patient.personId }}">
      <button type="submit">
        Run all {{ remaining }} remaining trial{{ remaining == 1 ? '' : 's' }}
      </button>
      {# The confirm() is an enhancement, not a guard — with JavaScript off it
         does not fire and the form posts. The action is idempotent in the ways
         that matter, so that is a trade rather than a hole. #}
    </form>
  {% elseif trials is not empty %}
    <p class="muted">This patient has an evaluation against every trial. Use “Re-run” on a row to run one again.</p>
  {% endif %}
</div>

{% if trials is empty %}
  {#
    Three genuinely different reasons the table can be empty, and telling them
    apart is the whole point of this block.

    A blank table on a screen headed "queue this patient against trials" gets
    read as a clinical statement — "nothing here suits this patient". That is
    only ONE of the three cases, and it is the only one where the reading is
    correct. Saying "No trials found" for all three would put a finding on
    screen that we did not make, twice out of three times.

    Ordered most-informative first, because `matchingFailed` is the only case
    where the trials list itself is untrustworthy.
  #}
  {% if matchingFailed %}
    <p class="muted">
      No trials found, and matching was unavailable — so this is a problem with
      the trials database rather than a finding about this patient. Check that it
      is reachable and that <code>TRIALS_DATABASE_URL</code> points at the right
      database.
    </p>
  {% else %}
    <p class="muted">
      <strong>No matching trials.</strong>
      EXACT compared {{ patient.displayName }} against every trial it holds and
      none were a match.
      {#
        The overwhelmingly common cause, worth naming rather than leaving to be
        rediscovered: the trials database is disease-specific. A myeloma patient
        against a breast-cancer corpus correctly matches nothing, and that looks
        identical to a broken integration unless the screen says so.
      #}
      {% if patient.disease %}
        This patient's recorded condition is <strong>{{ patient.disease }}</strong> —
        if the loaded trial set covers a different condition, no match is the
        expected result.
      {% endif %}
    </p>
  {% endif %}
{% else %}
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>
        {% if matchingFailed %}
          {{ trials|length }} trial{{ trials|length == 1 ? '' : 's' }},
          <strong>unranked</strong> — matching was unavailable, so this is every
          trial rather than the ones that suit this patient ·
          queue {{ patient.displayName }} against each one
        {% else %}
          {{ trials|length }} matching trial{{ trials|length == 1 ? '' : 's' }} for
          {{ patient.displayName }}, best match first
        {% endif %}
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Study</th>
          <th scope="col">Title</th>
          {#
            The match column is omitted entirely when matching failed, rather
            than rendered full of em dashes. An empty column implies the data
            exists and is unknown per-row; the truth is that we never got any of
            it, and the caption above says so once instead of 92 times.
          #}
          {% if not matchingFailed %}
            {# Numeric, so right-aligned and sortable by eye down the column. #}
            <th scope="col">Match</th>
          {% endif %}
          <th scope="col">Phase</th>
          <th scope="col">Sponsor</th>
          <th scope="col">Status</th>
          <th scope="col">Last Evaluated</th>
          <th scope="col"><span class="visually-hidden">Actions</span></th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for trial in trials %}
          {% set evaluation = latest[trial.id] ?? null %}
          {# The id is the redirect target after queueing, so the browser puts
             you back on this row instead of the top of the page. #}
          <tr id="trial-{{ trial.id }}">
            <th scope="row">
              {{ trial.studyId ?? '—' }}
              <span class="muted">({{ trial.id }})</span>
            </th>

            {# Twig's core `length` and `slice` are multibyte-aware (mb_strlen /
               mb_substr), so this cannot split a character in half the way PHP's
               substr() would. Same truncation as the trial picker. #}
            {% set title = trial.displayTitle %}
            <td>{{ title|length > 90 ? title|slice(0, 90) ~ '…' : title }}</td>

            {% if not matchingFailed %}
              {% set trialMatch = matches[trial.id] ?? null %}
              <td class="match-cell">
                {% if trialMatch is null %}
                  <span class="muted">—</span>
                {% else %}
                  {#
                    Score first — it is the number people scan down the column.
                    Explicitly tested for null rather than using ?? or |default:
                    matchScore is legitimately null when EXACT scored nothing,
                    and 0 is a real score. `0 ?? '—'` would print 0 correctly but
                    `0|default('—')` would print the dash, because 0 is falsy in
                    Twig. That is the same class of bug as ECOG 0 rendering as an
                    em dash, and it is why this is an `is null` test.
                  #}
                  {% if trialMatch.matchScore is not null %}
                    <strong>{{ trialMatch.matchScore }}</strong><span class="visually-hidden"> out of 100</span>
                  {% else %}
                    <span class="muted">unscored</span>
                  {% endif %}

                  {#
                    The verdict badge carries the WORD, not just a colour —
                    colour alone fails for colour-blind users and in forced-colours
                    mode. Same rule the evaluation status badge follows below.
                  #}
                  {% if trialMatch.matchingType %}
                    <span class="badge badge--match-{{ trialMatch.matchingType.cssModifier }}">{{ trialMatch.matchingType.label }}</span>
                  {% endif %}

                  {#
                    The actionable part of a "possible" match: what is missing
                    from ctomop. Named rather than counted, because "needs ECOG"
                    tells someone what to go and record while "3 gaps" does not.
                    <details> keeps a long list from crowding the row, and is
                    keyboard-operable with no JavaScript.
                  #}
                  {% if trialMatch.missingAttributeCount > 0 %}
                    {% set missing = trialMatch.missingAttributeTitles %}
                    <details class="match-gaps">
                      <summary>
                        {{ trialMatch.missingAttributeCount }} missing
                        <span class="visually-hidden">
                          patient attribute{{ trialMatch.missingAttributeCount == 1 ? '' : 's' }}
                          for {{ trial.studyId ?? ('trial ' ~ trial.id) }}
                        </span>
                      </summary>
                      <ul>
                        {% for title in missing %}<li>{{ title }}</li>{% endfor %}
                      </ul>
                    </details>
                  {% endif %}

                  {% if trialMatch.distanceLabel %}
                    <span class="muted">{{ trialMatch.distanceLabel }}</span>
                  {% endif %}
                {% endif %}
              </td>
            {% endif %}

            <td>{{ trial.phaseLabel ?? '—' }}</td>
            <td>{{ trial.sponsorName ?? '—' }}</td>

            <td>
              {% if evaluation is null %}
                <span class="muted">Not run</span>
              {% else %}
                {# The badge carries the word, not just a colour — colour alone
                   fails for colour-blind users and in high-contrast modes. #}
                <span class="badge badge--{{ evaluation.status.value }}">{{ evaluation.status.label }}</span>
              {% endif %}
            </td>

            <td>
              {% if evaluation is null %}
                <span class="muted"></span>
              {% else %}
                <span class="muted">{{ evaluation.ranAt ? evaluation.ranAt|date('Y-m-d H:i:s') : '—' }}</span>
                <a href="{{ path('lumina_ui_evaluation_show', {id: evaluation.id}) }}">
                  <span class="visually-hidden">View evaluation against {{ trial.studyId ?? trial.id }}, </span> View Result
                </a>
              {% endif %}
            </td>

            <td>
              {% if evaluation is null or evaluation.status.isTerminal %}
                <form method="post" action="{{ path('lumina_ui_evaluation_queue') }}" class="inline">
                  {# Token scoped to this trial AND this patient — the SAME scheme
                     the trial-first table uses, so both screens post forms the one
                     queue endpoint validates identically. #}
                  <input type="hidden" name="_token"
                         value="{{ csrf_token('queue-' ~ trial.id ~ '-' ~ patient.personId) }}">
                  <input type="hidden" name="trial_id" value="{{ trial.id }}">
                  <input type="hidden" name="person_id" value="{{ patient.personId }}">
                  {# Return here, not to the trial-first screen. This field affects
                     the redirect and nothing else. #}
                  <input type="hidden" name="flow" value="patient">
                  <button type="submit">
                    {{ evaluation is null ? 'Run' : 'Re-run' }}
                    {# 180 buttons all named "Queue" are impossible to tell apart
                       in a screen reader's element list. #}
                    <span class="visually-hidden">evaluation against {{ trial.studyId ?? ('trial ' ~ trial.id) }}</span>
                  </button>
                </form>
              {% else %}
                <span class="muted">
                  Running
                  <span class="visually-hidden">— no action available for {{ trial.studyId ?? ('trial ' ~ trial.id) }} while it runs</span>
                </span>
              {% endif %}
            </td>
          </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
{% endif %}
